{
"Code": "660",
"Name": "Check coil",
"Type": "Warning",
"Description": "Coil(s) acting suspicious.",
"Cause": "Solenoid coils mounted incorrectly.\nConnector plug(s) damaged.\nSolenoid coil(s) defective.\nX19 wires damaged or poorly spliced.\nX19 plug damaged.\nController defective.",
"Trouble shooting": "Check that the solenoid coils are mounted on the correct valves by viewing the naming on the wires.\nCheck that X19 is wired according to the wiring diagram posted on the inside of the controller cabinet door.\nUnplug the black connector plugs from the coils and check for moisture and corrosion. Replace the connector plug if necessary (item no. 818760B).\nMeasure resistance directly on the solenoid coils. The values should be above\nOhm. If below, replace the solenoid coil.\nMeasure resistance on the X19 terminals 1-2 for Veco, 3-4 for Vexp and 5-6 for Vhg. The values should be above 4.5 Ohm. If not, check the wires\nfrom X19 to each solenoid coil for damages or poor splices. Repair wires as necessary.\nActivate the 3 solenoid coils by going to the Service menu  , line S01 Manual operation. Change the value of line M01 Manual mode from AUTO to MANUAL. Go down to lines M06, M07 and M08, and set the values to 100%.\nUsing a multimeter, measure the output voltages on X19 terminals 1-2 for Veco, 3-4 for Vexp and 5-6 for Vhg. The values should be between 2.5 and\n6.5 V DC.\nIf no output voltage measured on X19, unplug X19 and measure directly on the controller. If still no voltage, replace the controller. Otherwise replace the X19 plug.\nSee service bulletin “00053 Controller output voltages” for further details.",
"Criteria": "Hardware feedback signal indicating trouble detected.",
"Controller action": "\nLog | X | Alarm | X | Alarm light | Off",
"Consequence": "If a coil, controlling a valve, is malfunctioning, cooling can be disabled adding risk to cargo.",
"Elimination": "Power cycle will inactivate alarm.",
"Log data": "Parm 1 | Parm 2 | Parm 3 | Parm 4 | Parm 5\nIf more than one coil fails, values are\naccumulated e.g. Veco and Vhg result in value 512 + 2048 = 2560."
},
{
"Code": "661",
"Name": "Check contactor",
"Type": "Warning",
"Description": "Contactor(s) acting suspicious.",
"Cause": "One or more contactors needs inspection.\nController output electronic defect.\nContactor close circuit.\nContactor open circuit.\nContactor coil.\nController output voltages.",
"Trouble shooting": "Measure output voltages (X16, X17, X18) with a multi-meter – OK range 2.5V to 6.5V. In case of a contactor coil is short circuited (hence the current draw will be too high), the controller voltages output will shut off and the measured output voltage is 0 Vdc.\nRepeat 1. with related contactor disconnected to check output.\nIf voltage is out of OK range, measure the resistance of the component connected to the “defective” controller output – OK range Contactor coil: approx. 5-6 Ω (Danfoss), 8-10 Ω (Schneider), 11-13 Ω (ABB).\nReplace components according to 1., 2. and 3.\nSee service bulletin “00053 Controller output voltages” for further information.",
"Criteria": "Hardware feedback signal indicating trouble detected.",
"Controller action": "\nLog | X | Alarm | X | Alarm light | Off",
"Consequence": "Depending on which contactor potentially is malfunctioning, cooling can be disabled adding risk to cargo.",
"Elimination": "Power cycle will inactivate alarm.",
"Log data": "Parm 1 | Parm 2 | Parm 3 | Parm 4 | Parm 5\nIf more than one contactor fails, values are accumulated e.g K2 CCW and K6 Mevap L result in value 2 + 32 = 34."
},
